Helping students experiencing homelessness succeed in school
The program assists students who are experiencing homelessness with school enrollment and with transportation arrangements to and from school. Many students face a variety of obstacles that hamper their ability to attend school.
Qualifications A student homeless if they are living in any of the following situations:
In a public or private place not designated as a regular sleeping accommodation (vehicle, park, hotel or campground)
Sneakers (new)
Make a request to speak with the Families in Transition team
Make a confidential request to speak with Families in Transition staff.
Absences should be reported to the teacher or the school office within three (3) days of the absence, listing the reason for the absence. Excuse notes may be submitted via ParentVUEor emailed to your child’s school building, using the school’s email address.
We understand that extenuating circumstances (childcare, connectivity issues) during at home and hybrid learning periods may prevent a child from being able to attend the live sessions. Should a student need to miss a live lesson period, parents are asked to contact the child’s teacher to avoid any miscommunication or missed work.
Missing school affects reading level, grades, graduation and future success.
Missing school in kindergarten or first grade has especially negative effects on reading and math skills and can affect students as they go on to later grades.
Before sending your children to school, we ask that families take their temperatures and check for any symptoms of illness, such as cough or runny nose.
Excuses like “I don’t feel like going to school today” are not good ones. The student should be encouraged to go.
Contact the Student and Family Resources Specialist at your child’s school. Our Specialists are trained to help parents improve their child’s attendance when life gets in the way.
