In the School District of Lancaster, we strive to provide ELA instruction that will prepare students to be effective readers, writers, and communicators in the 21 st century. Our ELA curriculum follows the targets provided by the PA Core Standards.
The chart below highlights some of the major concepts addressed in each domain across the elementary grade levels.
Asking and answering questions about text
Describing characters, settings, or events in a text
Writing the following types of text: Informational and explanatory texts; Narrative texts; Opinion and argumentative texts
Participate or engage in collaborative discussions
Includes key ideas and details, craft and structure, integration of knowledge and ideas, vocabulary acquisition and use, range of reading
Includes informative/explanatory writing, opinion/argumentative writing, narrative writing, response to literature, production and distribution of writing, technology and publication, conducting research, credibility, reliability, and validity of sources, range of writing
Includes comprehension and collaboration, presentation of knowledge and ideas, integration of knowledge and ideas, conventions of Standard English
Federal aid typically accounts for the most financial support in attending college. Federal aid includes:
Direct Unsubsidized Loans:Higher interest rates, but still lower than market rates. Interest accrues while enrolled but don’t have to pay back until after graduation.
529 college savings account are tax advantaged accounts to save for your or a relatives education. All account growth is untaxed which can provide long term benefits.
There are several college scholarships available exclusively for School District of Lancaster students.
MLK Jr. Memorial Fund:Private scholarships totaling $175,000 a year offered exclusively for McCaskey students
One exception is no-interest loans through the Lancaster Dollars for Higher Learning. Students can receive loans for up to four years that never accrue interest.
